DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Don’t  use  these  functions under Linux.  Due to a historical mistake,
       under Linux these functions are aliases for rraaiissee(3) and ssiiggnnaall(2), re‐
       spectively.

       Elsewhere, on System V‐like systems, these functions implement software
       signaling, entirely independent of the classical ssiiggnnaall(2) and  kkiillll(2)
       functions.   The function ssssiiggnnaall() defines the action to take when the
       software signal with number _s_i_g_n_u_m is raised using the  function  ggssiigg‐‐
       nnaall(),  and  returns the previous such action or SSIIGG__DDFFLL.  The function
       ggssiiggnnaall() does the following: if no action (or the action SSIIGG__DDFFLL)  was
       specified  for  _s_i_g_n_u_m, then it does nothing and returns 0.  If the ac‐
       tion SSIIGG__IIGGNN was specified for _s_i_g_n_u_m, then it does nothing and returns
       1.  Otherwise, it resets the action to SSIIGG__DDFFLL  and  calls  the  action
       function  with  argument _s_i_g_n_u_m, and returns the value returned by that
       function.  The range of possible values _s_i_g_n_u_m varies  (often  1–15  or
       1–17).

